@namespace AuthWithAdmin.Client.Pages.Settings
@using AuthWithAdmin.Shared.AuthSharedModels
@using AuthWithAdmin.Client.Services
@using AuthWithAdmin.Client.Components
@inject IUserProfileService UserProfile

@*
    StaffProfilePage — "הפרופיל שלי" for Lecturer, Mentor and Admin.

    STRUCTURAL SOURCE OF TRUTH: StudentProfilePage. The student screen is the
    approved composition — an identity row over a 1.6fr / 1fr grid, the wide
    column carrying what the user edits about themselves and the narrow one
    the read-mostly blocks — and this is that composition for the roles that
    were still being served the legacy /settings page.

        ┌ identity: avatar · name · role line ───────────────────────────┐
        ├──────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┤
        │ פרטים אישיים        (opaque)     │ העדפות           (translucent)│
        │ שירותים מחוברים    (translucent) │                              │
        └──────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┘

    ONE LAYOUT FOR ALL THREE ROLES. There is no card on this page that a
    lecturer has and a mentor does not, or the reverse, so the page has no
    role branch in it at all — see "WHAT IS NOT ON THIS PAGE" below.

    ── WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS AT ALL ──────────────────────────────────────────
    Lecturer, Mentor and Admin have been on the Motiva canvas shell since
    AppLayout's Shell.Staff branch stopped being reachable — GetShell sends
    every non-student to Shell.Lecturer or Shell.Mentor. What they were NOT
    given is a Motiva page inside it: /settings still rendered
    StaffSettingsPage, a legacy `spp-*` screen built on the `--g-*` tokens.
    Inside `.motiva-final` those tokens resolve to the right colours and the
    WRONG geometry — a documented trap in this codebase — so the screen read
    as a different product from every other page in the same shell.

    ── ADMIN IS HERE TOO, AND THAT IS NOT SCOPE CREEP ───────────────────────
    StaffSettingsPage's non-student branch made no distinction between a
    lecturer and an admin: same /api/users/me load, same fields, same cards.
    GetShell already sends both to the same shell for the same stated reason
    ("Admin no longer gets a separate legacy sidebar shell"). Leaving Admin
    behind would have meant keeping the legacy screen alive to serve a role
    that renders it identically to the one being migrated.

    ── WHAT EACH BLOCK READS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
      * identity + פרטים אישיים   GET /api/users/me (UserProfileDto), written
                                  back through PUT /api/users/me/profile
      * העדפות                    GET/PUT /api/users/me/notification-preferences
      * שירותים מחוברים           GET /api/google-calendar/status + the real
                                  OAuth connect / disconnect endpoints

    Every one of those three is per-ACCOUNT: one row per user, no project and
    no team anywhere in the key. That is the page's admission rule, and the
    section below is what it excludes.

    Two of those four are the STUDENT'S OWN COMPONENTS, rendered here rather
    than copied: ProfileNotificationsCard and ProfileIntegrationsCard sit in
    this folder now precisely because neither was ever a student's.

    ── WHAT IS NOT ON THIS PAGE: העדפות מנחה ────────────────────────────────
    A mentor's mentorship preferences — involvement level, preferred channel,
    expected response time, update cadence, red lines, meeting format — are
    NOT profile settings and this page does not render them.

    They are stated per TEAM. A mentor who wants a weekly written update from
    one project and "call me when you are stuck" from another is describing
    two working agreements, not changing their mind about one global one; the
    same is true of every field in that set. Published on a profile they read
    as one contract the mentor holds with everybody, which is the one thing
    they are not — and a student reading a value that was written with a
    different team in mind is worse served than a student who sees nothing.

    THE DATA AND THE COMPONENT ARE BOTH INTACT. Nothing was deleted, no
    endpoint changed, and MentorPreferencesCard is untouched on disk — it is
    simply no longer rendered by any routed page, because the page that used
    to render it (StaffSettingsPage) is unreferenced too. Re-homing it at the
    team / project level is deliberately not this task's; that area is being
    worked separately, and the values a mentor already saved are still read
    today by the mentor-profile popup and the team quick-info popover.

    Nothing was put in its place. The card is gone, not swapped for a stub or
    a "coming soon" — see the column note below for what happened to the
    space instead.

    ── WHAT ELSE IS NOT HERE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Reported, not silently dropped. Every one of these was student-gated on
    the legacy screen too, so no user of THIS page loses anything:

      * the avatar upload / remove overlay — `_isStudent`-gated there, and
        for a real reason: PUT/DELETE /api/student/me/avatar are the only
        avatar endpoints and they carry [Authorize(Roles = Student)]. See
        StaffProfileIdentityHero.
      * the Slack row — `_isStudent`-gated there; its audience is unchanged.
      * מראה (theme) and the five quick toggles — both write
        StudentPreferences through /api/student/me, student-only.

    And nothing was invented to fill the page. "General professional
    information" would be the natural fourth block for a lecturer or a mentor
    — a title, a department, a field of expertise — and the product stores
    none of it: users carries FirstName, LastName, Email, Phone,
    ProfileImagePath and the role rows, and that is the whole account.

    ── HOW THE TWO COLUMNS BALANCE ──────────────────────────────────────────
    The student's grid, with the two short cards paired against the tall one
    rather than stacked on top of it.

    שירותים מחוברים sits under פרטים אישיים in the WIDE column and העדפות
    takes the narrow one — which is a swap of the student page's own
    arrangement, and the reason is arithmetic rather than taste. The student
    has four cards, two per column, and they balance. Take away העדפות מנחה
    and this page has three: a four-field card (~230px), a seven-switch list
    (~330px) and a single service row (~120px). Stacked the student's way the
    wide column runs ~560px against a ~120px narrow one, and the last-card
    stretch rule would hand a lone Google Calendar row four hundred pixels of
    empty card — the exact ragged foot that rule exists to prevent, made
    worse. Paired this way the columns come out at ~350 and ~330, and the
    stretch has almost nothing left to absorb.

    Both cards are unharmed by the swap: שירותים מחוברים is a single flex row
    that simply gets wider, and העדפות is a list of "title … switch" rows,
    which is the shape the narrow track is for.

    Not routable: SettingsPage.razor owns /settings and picks between this and
    StudentProfilePage.
*@

@if (_loading)
{
    <div class="stf-page-state">
        <span class="stf-page-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>טוענת פרטים…</span>
    </div>
}
else if (_profile is null)
{
    <div class="stf-page-state">
        <MEmptyState Live="true"
                     Title="אירעה שגיאה בטעינת הפרופיל"
                     Description="נסי לרענן את הדף." />
    </div>
}
else
{
    <StaffProfileIdentityHero Profile="_profile" />

    <div class="stf-grid">
        @* Each column is a plain flex stack, as on the student page — the
           sections in a column are siblings at the same rhythm as the gap
           between the columns themselves.

           No role branch. Every card here is per-account, so a lecturer, a
           mentor and an admin see the same three. *@
        <div class="stf-col">
            <StaffProfilePersonalCard Profile="_profile"
                                      OnProfileChanged="StateHasChanged" />
            <ProfileIntegrationsCard />
        </div>

        <div class="stf-col">
            <ProfileNotificationsCard StudentVoice="false" />
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    private UserProfileDto? _profile;
    private bool _loading = true;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _profile = await UserProfile.GetMyAsync();
        _loading = false;
    }
}
